From: Gemini PDA Linux port <mratan/gemini-linux>
Subject: [PATCH] clk: mediatek: mt6797-img: size the provider by CLK_IMG_NR

clk-mt6797-img.c sizes its provider with ARRAY_SIZE(img_clks), which is
4. But the MT6797 imgsys clock IDs run 1..4 --

    CLK_IMG_FDVT   1
    CLK_IMG_DPE    2
    CLK_IMG_DIP    3
    CLK_IMG_LARB6  4
    CLK_IMG_NR     5

-- so the provider needs FIVE slots. With four it allocates indices 0..3
and any consumer asking for CLK_IMG_LARB6 gets -ENOENT. The clock can
never be handed out.

It went unnoticed because nothing in tree had ever requested an imgsys
clock on this SoC. The first consumer (a camera bridge on the Gemini PDA,
adding the IMG clocks the vendor HAL enables before touching any CAM
gate) failed to probe immediately:

    gemini-camsv 1a040000.camera-bridge: error -ENOENT: Failed to get clk 'img_larb6'

The camsys provider next door survives the identical expression only
because its IDs start at 0.

This is not Gemini-specific and applies to any MT6797 board.
---
--- a/drivers/clk/mediatek/clk-mt6797-img.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/mediatek/clk-mt6797-img.c
@@ -28,7 +28,18 @@
 
 static const struct mtk_clk_desc img_desc = {
 	.clks = img_clks,
-	.num_clks = ARRAY_SIZE(img_clks),
+	/*
+	 * CLK_IMG_NR, not ARRAY_SIZE(img_clks). The IDs here run 1..4
+	 * (CLK_IMG_FDVT is 1, CLK_IMG_LARB6 is 4) so the provider needs FIVE
+	 * slots; sizing it by the array length gives four and indices 0..3,
+	 * and any consumer asking for CLK_IMG_LARB6 gets -ENOENT. Nothing in
+	 * tree requested an imgsys clock on this SoC until now, which is why
+	 * it went unnoticed.
+	 *
+	 * The camsys provider next door gets away with the same expression
+	 * only because its IDs start at 0.
+	 */
+	.num_clks = CLK_IMG_NR,
 };
 
 static const struct of_device_id of_match_clk_mt6797_img[] = {
